    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm working on a low-fat mayo formulation and having issues with phase separation in high-acid environments. Can you explain the specific molecular mechanics of how lecithin acts as an emulsifier in this context, especially regarding the oil-water interface?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lecithin, a common emulsifier, acts at the oil-water interface by orienting its hydrophilic (water-attracting) and hydrophobic (oil-attracting) parts perpendicularly across the phase boundary. The hydrophilic phospholipid head groups face water, while the hydrophobic fatty acid chains point towards the oil, effectively stabilizing the emulsion. In acidic conditions, the molecular structure of lecithin may be affected, potentially impairing its emulsifying properties. Factors such as the concentration and the specific fatty acid composition of the lecithin can influence its performance in maintaining stability. Modifying the lecithin molecule or its environment pH could help overcome this challenge while maintaining the low-fat formulation's integrity.",
